-- Lesson 1: Below Zero
'{"hook":"For centuries, European mathematicians called negative numbers ''absurd'' and ''fictitious.'' Meanwhile, Chinese merchants had been using them for bookkeeping since 200 BCE — with red rods for positive and black rods for negative.","inventor":{"name":"Liu Hui","birth_year":225,"death_year":295,"nationality":"Chinese","bio":"A Chinese mathematician who, in his 263 CE commentary on The Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art, gave clear rules for adding and subtracting positive and negative numbers using colored counting rods."},"year_invented":200,"etymology":{"word":"negative","from":"Latin","parts":[{"root":"negativus","meaning":"that which denies"},{"root":"negare","meaning":"to deny, to say no"}]},"story_paragraphs":["The ancient Chinese used counting rods — small bamboo sticks — to do arithmetic. Red rods meant profit or gain; black rods meant debt or loss. By 200 BCE, they had a working system of negative numbers, centuries before anyone else.","In India, Brahmagupta (628 CE) wrote the first formal rules for negative number arithmetic: ''A debt minus a debt is a debt; a fortune minus a debt is a fortune.'' He treated them as fully legitimate numbers.","European mathematicians resisted. As late as 1758, the British mathematician Francis Maseres called negative numbers ''absurd'' and said they ''darken the very whole doctrines of equations.'' Even Descartes called negative solutions to equations ''false roots.''","Today negative numbers are essential: temperatures below zero, sea levels below the surface, bank overdrafts, elevations below ground, electrical charge, and coordinate systems. What was once ''absurd'' is now fundamental."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Brahmagupta","contribution":"Indian mathematician (628 CE) who formalized the rules for negative number arithmetic"},{"name":"Fibonacci","contribution":"Introduced negative numbers to European mathematics through his 1202 book Liber Abaci"},{"name":"John Wallis","contribution":"English mathematician who in 1685 placed negative numbers on the number line, giving them geometric meaning"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Temperature","icon":"🌡️","description":"Below-zero temperatures: -10°C means 10 degrees below freezing"},{"name":"Money","icon":"💳","description":"Bank accounts can go negative — an overdraft of $50 means a balance of -$50"},{"name":"Elevation","icon":"🏔️","description":"The Dead Sea is at -430 meters — 430 meters below sea level"},{"name":"Sports","icon":"⛳","description":"Golf scores: -3 (three under par) is better than +2 (two over par)"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Chinese counting rods showing positive (red) and negative (black) numbers","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":null}}'::jsonb)
